---
/**
 * Privacy policy.
 *
 * Written from an audit of the code rather than from a template, because a
 * policy that describes collection which does not happen is as wrong as one
 * that hides collection which does. What the audit found:
 *
 *   - localStorage holds exactly one key, `theme` (Layout.astro reads it before
 *     first paint, Navbar.astro writes it). The work-week choice is NOT stored;
 *     it lives in memory for the page view only.
 *   - Cookies: Google Analytics 4 sets `_ga` and `_ga_BR986NK1GM`. Nothing in
 *     src/ writes `document.cookie` directly; these come from gtag.js.
 *   - Analytics: GA4, added 2026-08-26, loaded on every page from
 *     Layout.astro. No tag manager, no ad script, no social pixel.
 *   - No fetch/XHR to any first-party endpoint. There is no backend.
 *   - Third-party origins the browser really does contact: Google Fonts on every
 *     page, and on /company-optimizer only, cdnjs (pdf.js) and jsDelivr
 *     (tesseract.js, which pulls its English model at runtime). Both libraries
 *     are version-pinned and SRI-hashed — see VENDOR_SCRIPTS in
 *     company-optimizer.astro — which fixes WHICH code runs but does not remove
 *     the request, so the list below is unchanged by it.
 *
 * Those last ones are the part a boilerplate policy would have missed, and they
 * are the only way an IP address of yours reaches a third party. If a script,
 * font host or storage key is ever added, this page is wrong until it is edited.
 */
import DocPage from "../layouts/DocPage.astro";

const TITLE = "Privacy Policy | The Long Weekends";
const DESCRIPTION =
  "The Long Weekends has no accounts and no ads, and never uploads your holiday list. Exactly which cookies Google Analytics sets, what it measures, and how to opt out.";
---

<DocPage
  title={TITLE}
  description={DESCRIPTION}
  path="/privacy"
  eyebrow="Legal"
  heading="Privacy policy"
  lede="The short version: no accounts, no ads, and nothing you type here is ever uploaded. Google Analytics measures which pages get visited and sets two cookies to do it — how to switch that off is below."
  updated="2026-08-26"
  numbered
>
  <h2>Who this is</h2>
  <p>
    The Long Weekends is an independent project operated by an individual based in
    India ("we", "us"). It is not a registered company. You can reach us at <a href="mailto:hello@thelongweekends.com">hello@thelongweekends.com</a>, which
    is also the address for any question about this policy.
  </p>

  <h2>What we do not collect</h2>
  <p>
    Stated plainly, because most of this policy is about absences rather than
    practices. We do not:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>ask you to create an account, or collect a name, phone number or address;</li>
    <li>
      set any advertising cookie, or share what you do here with an ad network;
    </li>
    <li>show advertising, or embed any advertising or social media tracking pixel;</li>
    <li>
      upload your holiday list — the calculator and the office optimizer both run
      entirely in your browser;
    </li>
    <li>have a mailing list, and so cannot add you to one;</li>
    <li>
      operate any server that receives your data — the site is a set of static
      files, with no database and no backend to send anything to.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    There is no form anywhere on this site. Nothing you type into the calculator or
    the office optimizer is transmitted, because there is nowhere for it to be
    transmitted to.
  </p>

  <h2>What is stored on your device</h2>
  <p>
    One item that we set, in your browser's <strong>local storage</strong> — not a
    cookie, so it is never attached to a network request and never sent to us or to
    anyone else:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong><code>theme</code></strong> — the value <code>"light"</code> or <code>"dark"</code>, written when you use the theme toggle. It exists so the
      site does not flash the wrong colour scheme on your next visit.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    That is the whole list of what <em>we</em> store. Your region and work-week
    choices are deliberately <em>not</em> kept: they last for the page view and
    reset when you leave. Google Analytics separately sets two cookies, described
    in the next section. To remove all of it, clear site data for this domain in
    your browser settings, or use private browsing, where nothing persists at all.
  </p>

  <h2>Analytics</h2>
  <p>
    This site uses <strong>Google Analytics 4</strong> to count page views. It was
    added in August 2026; before that the site measured nothing at all, and earlier
    versions of this page said so.
  </p>
  <p>What it records on each page view:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      which page you looked at, when, and which site or search you arrived from;
    </li>
    <li>
      your approximate location, derived by Google from your IP address — country
      and city level, not an address;
    </li>
    <li>your device type, screen size, browser and operating system;</li>
    <li>
      a randomly generated identifier that lets Google tell a returning browser
      from a new one. It is not tied to a name, an email or an account, because we
      hold none of those.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    To do that it sets two cookies on this domain, <code>_ga</code> and
    <code>_ga_BR986NK1GM</code>. They contain the random identifier and a
    timestamp, and Google's default retention for them is two years.
  </p>
  <p>
    We use this to see which pages people actually reach and which are dead ends —
    nothing more. <strong>Your holiday list is never part of it.</strong> The file
    you upload to the office optimizer is read in your browser and never leaves it,
    and no date you enter is sent to Google or to us.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>If you would rather not be counted</strong>, any of these works: install
    Google's official <a
      href="https://tools.google.com/dlpage/gaoptout"
      rel="nofollow noopener"
      target="_blank">Analytics opt-out add-on</a>, block cookies for this domain in
    your browser settings, or use any content blocker — nearly all of them block
    this script by default. The calculator works exactly the same either way; none
    of its features depend on analytics loading.
  </p>

  <h2>Your office holiday list stays with you</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="/company-optimizer">office holiday optimizer</a> accepts pasted
    text, PDFs and images of your company's holiday list. All of it —
    reading the file, extracting dates, running the optimiser — happens <strong>inside your browser</strong>. The file is never uploaded, never
    transmitted, and no copy of it reaches us or any third party.
  </p>
  <p>
    We therefore have no ability to see your company's holidays, and no copy to
    hand over, lose, or be compelled to disclose. Closing the tab is all it takes
    to discard the data.
  </p>

  <h2>Third parties your browser contacts</h2>
  <p>
    Beyond the analytics above, the site loads resources from other people's
    servers. Any HTTP request necessarily reveals your <strong>IP address</strong>, <strong>browser and operating system</strong>, and the page you were on, to
    whoever serves it. This is the complete list:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Google Analytics</strong> (<code>googletagmanager.com</code>,
      <code>google-analytics.com</code>) — the page-view counting described above.
      Loaded on every page.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Google Fonts</strong> (<code>fonts.googleapis.com</code>, <code>fonts.gstatic.com</code>) — the two typefaces the site is set in. Loaded
      on every page. Google's handling of these requests is governed by its own
      privacy policy.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>cdnjs</strong> (Cloudflare) — the PDF reader used to open a holiday
      list. Loaded <em>only</em> on the office optimizer page.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>jsDelivr</strong> — the text-recognition engine for scanned or
      image-based holiday lists, which also downloads its English language model the
      first time you run it. Loaded <em>only</em> on the office optimizer page.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    None of these receive the contents of your holiday list. They serve code and
    fonts, or count a page view; the planning work is done locally. If you visit
    only the national calendar, the two Google services above are the only third
    parties involved.
  </p>
  <p>
    The two libraries are requested at a <strong>fixed version</strong> and with a
    cryptographic fingerprint of the exact file we expect. If either server ever
    returned different code — through compromise or simple re-publication — your
    browser would refuse to run it rather than run it silently. That protects the
    code, not the connection: the requests above still happen, and still reveal what
    any HTTP request reveals.
  </p>

  <h2>Server logs</h2>
  <p>
    The static files are served by a hosting provider, which keeps standard access
    logs — IP address, timestamp, requested URL, browser string — for security and
    operational purposes. This is ordinary web-server behaviour, retained by the host
    under its own terms rather than at our request. We do not analyse those logs to
    build any profile of you.
  </p>

  <h2>If you email us</h2>
  <p>
    Writing to us means we hold your email address and whatever you chose to put in
    the message, in a mailbox, for as long as the correspondence is useful — typically
    until a reported bug or wrong date is fixed. It is not added to any list and not
    used to contact you about anything other than your message. Please do not include
    sensitive personal information; a holiday date correction never needs any.
  </p>

  <h2>Children</h2>
  <p>
    The site is a holiday calculator and is not directed at children. Because it
    collects nothing from anyone, it collects nothing from children either.
  </p>

  <h2>Your rights</h2>
  <p>
    Indian data protection law, including the <strong>Digital Personal Data
    Protection Act, 2023</strong>, gives you rights over personal data held about
    you — access, correction and erasure among them. In practice the only personal
    data we can hold is an email you sent us, so a request to delete it is a request
    to delete that correspondence, and we will do so on request.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <code>theme</code> value is under your control, not ours: it sits in your
    browser and you can clear it at any time without asking us.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
  <p>
    If a script, font host or stored value is ever added to the site, this page gets
    updated in the same change, and the date at the top moves. There is no
    notification mechanism, because there is no mailing list — the date is the record.
  </p>
</DocPage>
